WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.004
Imagine a former commercial site in Torrance proposed for mixed-use redevelopment.

2
00:00:06.004 --> 00:00:10.440
The plan includes homes, shops, public space, and new streetscape improvements.

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.539
At the hearing, one speaker says the project will transform the tax base.

4
00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:18.077
Another says new residents will cost more than they contribute.

5
00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:19.874
Both claims may sound confident.

6
00:00:19.874 --> 00:00:22.513
Neither is useful without a transparent method.

7
00:00:22.513 --> 00:00:31.497
Development fiscal impact analysis estimates how a project may change city revenues, service costs, capital needs, and risk compared with a no-project baseline.

8
00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:33.741
This lesson is educational preparation.

9
00:00:33.741 --> 00:00:40.071
It is not financial, legal, tax, appraisal, accounting, investment, real estate, or municipal advisory advice.

10
00:00:40.071 --> 00:00:44.502
California property tax allocation and development finance are fact specific.

11
00:00:44.502 --> 00:00:52.212
Use current records and qualified city finance staff, assessors, economists, appraisers, engineers, and counsel for an actual project.

12
00:00:52.212 --> 00:00:55.377
Start with how property tax works at a practical level.

13
00:00:55.377 --> 00:01:00.556
California's system is based on assessed value, not simply current market value each year.

14
00:01:00.556 --> 00:01:04.181
Change of ownership and new construction can affect assessment.

15
00:01:04.181 --> 00:01:10.971
The county collects the basic property tax and distributes revenue among local taxing entities under allocation rules.

16
00:01:10.971 --> 00:01:14.826
A city receives only its applicable share, not the entire tax bill.

17
00:01:14.826 --> 00:01:16.668
That point changes the analysis.

18
00:01:16.668 --> 00:01:22.364
Never multiply a project's estimated value by a headline tax rate and call the result city revenue.

19
00:01:22.364 --> 00:01:28.694
Identify the taxable assessed value, timing, exemptions or exclusions, allocation share, and possible appeals.

20
00:01:28.694 --> 00:01:31.514
Confirm the method with authoritative local data.

21
00:01:31.514 --> 00:01:34.391
Separate the existing baseline from the increment.

22
00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:41.066
If a site already produces revenue and requires service, the relevant question is the change caused by the proposal.

23
00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:46.187
Estimate existing city property tax, sales-related revenue, charges, and service demands.

24
00:01:46.187 --> 00:01:49.121
Then estimate the same categories with the project.

25
00:01:49.121 --> 00:01:51.653
The difference is incremental fiscal impact.

26
00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:54.876
Use stabilized conditions and the path to stabilization.

27
00:01:54.876 --> 00:01:57.695
A development may take years to build and occupy.

28
00:01:57.695 --> 00:02:02.011
Construction can generate temporary receipts and temporary service demands.

29
00:02:02.011 --> 00:02:05.578
Those should not be mistaken for permanent annual performance.

30
00:02:05.578 --> 00:02:11.045
Show entitlement, construction, lease-up, stabilization, and later renewal as different phases.

31
00:02:11.045 --> 00:02:26.088
On the revenue side, consider property tax, sales and use tax where applicable, transient occupancy tax if relevant, utility user taxes if applicable, business-related taxes, franchise revenues, permits, service charges, and other lawful sources.

32
00:02:26.088 --> 00:02:29.880
Do not add a category merely because another city receives it.

33
00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.610
Confirm Torrance authority, allocation, and fund destination.

34
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:48.531
On the cost side, identify police, fire and emergency medical response, transportation operations, street and signal maintenance, parks, libraries, community services, planning, code enforcement, finance, technology, and general administration.

35
00:02:48.531 --> 00:02:50.916
Some costs vary directly with activity.

36
00:02:50.916 --> 00:02:52.323
Some increase in steps.

37
00:02:52.323 --> 00:03:01.618
One additional resident does not require a fraction of a fire station, but enough cumulative growth may require a new unit, vehicle, facility, or shift.

38
00:03:01.618 --> 00:03:05.104
That is the difference between average and marginal cost.

39
00:03:05.104 --> 00:03:10.607
Average cost divides total current cost by a population, household, call, or service unit.

40
00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:15.071
It is easy to compute but can misstate the near-term effect of a project.

41
00:03:15.071 --> 00:03:18.740
Marginal cost estimates the added cost caused by the change.

42
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.758
It requires operational evidence.

43
00:03:20.758 --> 00:03:25.590
Ask departments what workload driver matters and where capacity thresholds sit.

44
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.586
Capital impact is separate from operating impact.

45
00:03:28.586 --> 00:03:34.212
Development may require intersections, utilities, drainage, parks, facilities, or equipment.

46
00:03:34.212 --> 00:03:37.147
Some improvements may be built by the developer.

47
00:03:37.147 --> 00:03:43.262
Some may be funded through lawful fees, assessments, district mechanisms, grants, or city resources.

48
00:03:43.262 --> 00:03:49.072
Identify who owns the asset, who pays initially, who maintains it, and when replacement occurs.

49
00:03:49.072 --> 00:03:50.295
Avoid fiscal zoning.

50
00:03:50.295 --> 00:04:01.180
A positive net revenue estimate is not a complete land-use finding, and a project with public costs may still advance housing, safety, equity, environmental, or legal objectives.

51
00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:09.191
Fiscal information should illuminate tradeoffs, not displace the general plan, housing law, environmental review, or public values.

52
00:04:09.191 --> 00:04:11.879
Build the model in five sheets or sections.

53
00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:13.066
First, assumptions.

54
00:04:13.066 --> 00:04:19.691
Include units, square footage, value, occupancy, phasing, inflation, service population, and source dates.

55
00:04:19.691 --> 00:04:20.754
Second, baseline.

56
00:04:20.754 --> 00:04:30.004
Record what occurs if the proposal is not approved, including existing revenue, existing service cost, likely vacancy, and any required maintenance.

57
00:04:30.004 --> 00:04:31.942
Third, annual operating impact.

58
00:04:31.942 --> 00:04:35.567
Estimate incremental recurring revenues and costs by fund.

59
00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:38.005
Fourth, capital and replacement impact.

60
00:04:38.005 --> 00:04:42.255
Show improvements, funding responsibility, useful life, and renewal.

61
00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:44.505
Fifth, sensitivity and distribution.

62
00:04:44.505 --> 00:04:51.880
Change the assumptions that matter most, and show which funds, neighborhoods, or service users experience the effects.

63
00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.255
Here is a hypothetical example, with invented numbers only for method.

64
00:04:56.255 --> 00:05:03.005
A redevelopment is assumed to add two hundred homes and twenty thousand square feet of local-serving retail.

65
00:05:03.005 --> 00:05:05.506
Do not assign a property tax amount yet.

66
00:05:05.506 --> 00:05:10.943
First obtain a defensible assessed-value estimate and the city's applicable allocation.

67
00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:18.568
Then estimate stabilized occupancy and taxable sales, avoiding the assumption that every retail dollar is new to Torrance.

68
00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:21.506
Some spending may shift from an existing store.

69
00:05:21.506 --> 00:05:28.444
For costs, ask whether current patrol, fire response, street maintenance, and recreation systems have capacity.

70
00:05:28.444 --> 00:05:36.069
Suppose staff identifies no immediate facility expansion but expects added annual calls and a signal upgrade in year four.

71
00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:36.882
Include both.

72
00:05:36.882 --> 00:05:44.569
Then test lower assessed value, slower occupancy, retail substitution, higher labor cost, and an earlier capital threshold.

73
00:05:44.569 --> 00:05:46.944
Report a range, not one magic surplus.

74
00:05:46.944 --> 00:05:57.195
Show the annual General Fund effect, effects on restricted or enterprise funds, one-time receipts, city-funded capital, developer-funded capital, and unfunded risk.

75
00:05:57.195 --> 00:06:01.195
State which results depend on negotiated terms or future action.

76
00:06:01.195 --> 00:06:03.971
Practical review begins with source discipline.

77
00:06:03.971 --> 00:06:12.536
For Torrance facts, use current city budgets, Capital Improvement Plans, public agenda reports, development agreements, fee schedules, and ACFRs.

78
00:06:12.536 --> 00:06:18.206
County assessor or auditor-controller information may be necessary for property tax assumptions.

79
00:06:18.206 --> 00:06:22.695
Cite each input by document, page or table, fiscal year, and retrieval date.

80
00:06:22.695 --> 00:06:25.707
Ask staff to reconcile the forecast after approval.

81
00:06:25.707 --> 00:06:31.436
Compare actual valuation, occupancy, receipts, calls for service, and maintenance with the model.

82
00:06:31.436 --> 00:06:33.563
Update the model when phases change.

83
00:06:33.563 --> 00:06:37.934
Fiscal impact analysis is more credible when it becomes a learning system.

84
00:06:37.934 --> 00:06:42.068
Your learner artifact is a Development Fiscal Impact Workbook Outline.

85
00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:44.726
It need not contain actual confidential data.

86
00:06:44.726 --> 00:06:47.089
Create the five sections just described.

87
00:06:47.089 --> 00:06:51.223
Add an assumption register with owner, source, range, and update date.

88
00:06:51.223 --> 00:06:57.957
Add a double-counting check for baseline growth, shifted sales, pass-through funds, reimbursements, and transfers.

89
00:06:57.957 --> 00:07:04.572
Finish with a one-paragraph conclusion that identifies the two assumptions most capable of reversing the result.

90
00:07:04.572 --> 00:07:06.226
Now, three recall questions.

91
00:07:06.226 --> 00:07:10.065
Question one: Why is the full property tax bill not city revenue?

92
00:07:10.065 --> 00:07:18.689
Answer: The county distributes property tax among multiple taxing entities under allocation rules, so the city receives only its applicable share.

93
00:07:18.689 --> 00:07:21.701
Question two: Why can average service cost mislead?

94
00:07:21.701 --> 00:07:25.304
Answer: Many services have available capacity and step costs.

95
00:07:25.304 --> 00:07:30.502
The fiscal effect depends on marginal workload and when a capacity threshold is crossed.

96
00:07:30.502 --> 00:07:34.400
Question three: What comparison defines incremental fiscal impact?

97
00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:41.547
Answer: The projected revenues, costs, and capital needs with the project compared with a defensible no-project baseline.

98
00:07:41.547 --> 00:07:54.993
This episode was informed by the publicly available module list for the Lincoln Institute of Land Policy course Foundations of Local Government Finance in the United States, including its property tax topic.

99
00:07:54.993 --> 00:07:59.735
No account-gated Lincoln lesson was accessed or represented as extracted.

100
00:07:59.735 --> 00:08:05.775
The public CDIAC Elect>Ed outline was used only for broad awareness of long-term commitments.

101
00:08:05.775 --> 00:08:07.984
No gated Elect>Ed lesson was used.

102
00:08:07.984 --> 00:08:23.443
The model structure adapts general revenue, expenditure, asset, and performance-management ideas from Municipal Finances: A Handbook for Local Governments, Farvacque-Vitkovic and Kopanyi, editors, World Bank, 2014, licensed CC BY 3.0 IGO.

103
00:08:23.443 --> 00:08:33.836
This adaptation replaces international examples with original California cautions, a Torrance hypothetical, marginal-cost analysis, and a five-section workbook.

104
00:08:33.836 --> 00:08:36.954
The World Bank does not endorse this adaptation.

105
00:08:36.954 --> 00:08:51.504
Additional grounding comes from Institute for Local Government explanations of California municipal revenues and limited-purpose funds, and from City of Torrance public budgets, CIPs, agenda materials, dashboards, and ACFRs.

106
00:08:51.504 --> 00:08:55.531
No Torrance figure in the hypothetical is presented as actual.

107
00:08:55.531 --> 00:08:58.259
A useful fiscal impact analysis is humble.

108
00:08:58.259 --> 00:09:07.743
It separates baseline from increment, city share from total tax, temporary activity from stable performance, and direct cost from future capacity.

109
00:09:07.743 --> 00:09:11.619
Next, we turn to economic development and land value capture.
